    "title": "Six Somali Judges File Suit at East African Court Over Chief Justice Dismissal",
    "original_title": "Six Somali Supreme Court Judges Take Government to East African Court Over Chief Justice&#8217;s Dismissal",
    "slug": "six-somali-judges-file-suit-at-east-african-court-over-chief-justice-dismissal",
    "summary": "Six Somali Supreme Court judges have taken the federal government to the East African Court of Justice, contesting the removal of Chief Justice Bashe Yusuf Ahmed and the ensuing gridlock at the country's top court.",
    "content": "Six judges from Somalia's Supreme Court have lodged a case against the Federal Republic of Somalia at the East African Court of Justice (EACJ) in Arusha, Tanzania. The suit challenges the dismissal of Chief Justice Bashe Yusuf Ahmed and argues that the removal has left the Supreme Court unable to function. The filing marks an escalation in a long-running dispute over judicial independence in Somalia.\n\nThe case stems from the removal of Chief Justice Ahmed, a decision that has been contested by several judges who argue it violated constitutional procedures. The six judges are seeking redress at the regional court, arguing that the dismissal and the subsequent paralysis of the Supreme Court undermine the rule of law in the country. The Somali government has not publicly commented on the filing.\n\nThe EACJ is the judicial arm of the East African Community, of which Somalia became a member in 2024. The court has jurisdiction to hear cases involving the interpretation and application of the EAC Treaty, including allegations of violations of fundamental rights and principles of good governance. This is one of the first cases brought by Somali citizens against their government at the regional court since Somalia's accession.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the case carries significant implications. Many diaspora members have family and business interests in Somalia and rely on a functioning judiciary to resolve disputes and uphold contracts. A weakened Supreme Court could erode confidence in the country's legal system and discourage investment. Additionally, the diaspora has historically been a key source of financial support for judicial reform efforts, and the outcome of this case may influence future engagement.\n\nThe filing also highlights broader concerns about the separation of powers in Somalia, where political tensions have often spilled over into the judiciary. The international community has repeatedly urged Somali authorities to strengthen judicial independence as part of state-building efforts. The EACJ's handling of the case will be closely watched as a test of regional mechanisms for accountability.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/six-somali-supreme-court-judges-take-government-to-east-african-court-over-chief-justices-dismissal/427832/",
